      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the concept of graph theory. We can represent the men and women as vertices of a graph, and the dances as edges between the vertices. Since each man danced with exactly three women, we can say that each vertex has a degree of 3. Similarly, since each woman danced with exactly two men, we can say that each vertex has a degree of 2.\n\nThe sum of the degrees of all the vertices in a graph is equal to twice the number of edges. In this case, we have 12 men and 12 women, so the sum of the degrees of all the vertices is 24. Since each vertex has a degree of either 2 or 3, we can set up the following equation:\n\n2 * number of vertices with degree 2 + 3 * number of vertices with degree 3 = 24\n\nLet x be the number of vertices with degree 2 and y be the number of vertices with degree 3. We can rewrite the equation as:\n\n2x + 3y = 24\n\nSince each vertex represents a person, we know that x + y = 12. We can solve this system of equations to find the values of x and y:\n\n2x + 3y = 24\nx + y = 12\n\nMultiplying the second equation by 2, we get:\n\n2x + 2y = 24\n\nSubtracting the first equation from this new equation, we get:\n\ny = 0\n\nSince y = 0, we can substitute this value into the second equation to find x:\n\nx + 0 = 12\nx = 12\n\nSo there are 12 vertices with degree 2 and 0 vertices with degree 3. Since each vertex with degree 2 represents a woman, there are 12 women at the party.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{12}.",
